   "body": "**mac/mac-mbp**: #934 not reproduced on the Mac at dev-swift 38f8081b (includes #958).\n\n- Setup: iPhone 18 Pro clone, iOS 27.2 simulator, one `build-for-testing` (60 s), then `test-without-building` with `-parallel-testing-enabled NO` (same as CI), `-only-testing` on the 15 reader web suites (74 tests: ReaderLandingWebTests, ReaderPageEndWebTests, ReaderWebControllerTests, Insets, Presence, Visibility, WebKitResetWatch*, ResetAgreement, PlusButton, Separation, Margins, LegacyStyle, ImageBaseline, SignatureLine, WebSecurity).\n- 20 consecutive runs: 20 pass, 0 fail. Run 1 (cold boot) 100 s, runs 2-20 51-56 s each.\n- Under CPU stress (12 `yes` processes on a 10-core Mac, load average well above core count): 6 further runs, 6 pass, 0 fail (84 s cold, then 54-57 s).\n- No code change, so no PR. The acceptance criterion (20 consecutive green runs) is met locally; the CI failures (jobs 133090341114, 133160363316, 133712438518, 133717388761) are not explained by it.\n- What CI has that the Mac does not: the `xcode-27` runner (a shared, queued runner whose simulator clone competes with other jobs; ci.yml:199-206 already records that clones starve each other there), a freshly created simulator per job and a `build test` in one go with the whole 16-minute suite running before and after these tests (so memory and WebContent processes are warm and loaded), while the Mac runs only the web suites on a warm device. I could not reproduce that locally. If it recurs, the next step is a CI run with the reader web suites alone, or the `.xcresult` of a failing job for the WebContent log.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_\n",
